I think this PR and the related Jira issue can be closed. The test coverage of bookmarks_controller is 99.51% on master, the only uncovered line is a [very generic error|https://app.codecov.io/gh/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/master/app%2Fcontrollers%2Fbookmarks_controller.rb#L219].{quote}

01/Jan/21 10:16 AM;557058:fa089759-8cd1-4db3-b506-5731a37fd5a6;{quote}If you delete a comment in this position, it [redirects you to the parent comment|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/b850d356002a00c4b57a679f64baf03662bf629a/app/controllers/comments_controller.rb#L329-L331].{quote}

Actually, the {{redirect_to_comment}} method is not guaranteed to redirect to the right comment unless the comment is deeply nested – otherwise, it just redirects to the first page of comments, like the spam button. So making the spam button behave more like the delete button is not the right thing to do.

(This is the other half of [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-2520|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-2520|smart-link] – the first half being the {{link_to}}/{{button_to}} issue.)

column150:
13/Sep/20 10:35 PM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;{quote}So to prevent being able to reply to a spam comment, I was thinking about maybe adding a validation that checks that the parent is not spam. But I think this validation would need to be scoped (only for creation? is that possible?), otherwise there would be a validation error when trying to edit or possibly even delete a comment that's replying to a parent comment that was marked as spam sometime after the fact.{quote}

It is\! You can use {{on: create}}. Here’s [the documentation for the on option for validations|https://guides.rubyonrails.org/active_record_validations.html#on].

{quote}I'm not sure what to do about the check_for_spam validation. Is it desirable behaviour, rerunning a spam check on a guest comment every time someone replies to it?{quote}

Yikes, that’s definitely not desirable. It should probably only run on creation. (Logged out comments can’t be edited, so we don’t need to worry about rechecking when the comment’s content is changed.)

