column154:
02/Apr/21 5:28 PM;557058:f99670ac-6f0b-469b-bdfb-a9817a67932f;Sarken:

{quote}There were 7458 batches, queued twice (once for the works index, once for the work creator index). \[…] there are 7464 batches left in the queue, so we can probably safely say it takes about 2 hours to reindex the 7.4 million works in their entirety.{quote}

{{rake search:reindex_works}} creates an index queue that did take about 2h to fully process.

{quote}There are 285 batches of external works, 521 batches of series, and 5650 batches of works \[…]{quote}

{{rake search:reindex_bookmarkables}} -creates a queue that- took at least 36h, because of the way we are [eager-loading bookmarked works|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/f6e1b98aa472c0777cf2140064218c1d5a8c87c5/app/models/search/bookmarked_work_indexer.rb#L8].

column155:
02/Apr/21 7:36 PM;557058:fa089759-8cd1-4db3-b506-5731a37fd5a6;{quote}{{rake search:reindex_bookmarkables}} creates a queue that took at least 36h, because of the way we are [eager-loading bookmarked works|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/f6e1b98aa472c0777cf2140064218c1d5a8c87c5/app/models/search/bookmarked_work_indexer.rb#L8].{quote}

I’m confused. Do you mean the rake task took 36 hours to run? Because the eager-loading for bookmarked works only affects the rake task’s runtime, not the time to process the queued batches. (The rake task {{search:reindex_bookmarkables}} calls {{index_from_db}}, which [sends only the IDs to AsyncIndexer|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/f6e1b98aa472c0777cf2140064218c1d5a8c87c5/app/models/search/indexer.rb#L127]. So by the time it actually processes the batches, I don’t believe it matters whether the works were loaded with {{includes(:stat_counter)}} in the original rake task, because they’re reloaded from the IDs later.)

(Actually, the indexing batches might run faster if they did have some {{includes}} statements. Currently indexing requires 10,000+ separate database queries for a full batch of 1000 works, which seems inefficient.)

