column30:
*Steps to reproduce*

# Log in as an admin 
# Invitations > Invite New Users
# Use the "Give invite codes to current users" section to mass grant invitations, which will trigger an email

*What happens*

The body of the HTML email is like:

{quote}<p>We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at <a style="color:#990000" href="http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations">your invitations page</a>.</p>{quote}

{{<p><%= t(".body", count: @total, invitation_page: style_link(t(".invitation_page_link_text"), user_invitations_url(@user))).html_safe %></p>}}

The body of the text email is like:

{quote}We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations.{quote}

{{<%= t(".body", count: @total, invitation_page: user_invitations_url(@user)) %>}}

*What should happen*

The text email should be like: 

{quote}We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at your invitations page (http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations).{quote}

Both emails should use our [i18n standards|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/wiki/Internationalization-(i18n)-Standards] for naming keys with URLs or links.

*Notes*

This isn't just pedantry; the current version causes issues in languages that don't use spaces. From Rhine:

{quote}As far as I can tell, the Increased Invites mail has %\{invitation_page} as a placeholder in the text and turns it into a hyperlink with something like "your Invitations page" in the HTML version and just the URL in the plain text version.

Since you wouldn't insert a space between the placeholder and the rest of the sentence in Chinese, there isn't one in the plain text version either - meaning that the program you use to display the plain text mail may include the text that follows as part of the URL as well, leading to a faulty link when directly going through that rather than copying the URL. {quote}

