    },
  });
}{code}

The check there passed for all requests, and I also saw {{Retry-After}} in the request header logs. Looks good to me as well\!

column30:
Currently, for feature tests we use this Capybara setting:

{code:ruby}Capybara.configure do |config|
  config.ignore_hidden_elements = false
end{code}

This means matchers can find text and elements not visible to users, including:

* With JS enabled, the original inputs that get hidden and replaced by jquery.tokeninput.js;
* With JS enabled, autocomplete inputs that allow max 1 token and get hidden if there’s already a value (“Synonym of”);
* CSS text inside {{<style>}} elements;
* Page title inside {{<title>}} elements;
* Error messages in JS live validation code, even if the error is not actually present.

Furthermore, [the notion of “visible” differs|https://makandracards.com/makandra/7617-change-how-capybara-sees-or-ignores-hidden-elements] between the Rack::Test driver (for non-JS tests) and drivers using a browser (Poltergeist + PhantomJS which we use, or Selenium + Chrome/Firefox).

Let’s remove this setting (default is true).

Also update to Capybara 3.x in the process.

There are a number of unused methods in [app/helpers/application_helper.rb|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/master/app/helpers/application_helper.rb]:

* {{link_to_remote}}
* {{allowed_css_instructions}}
* {{field_with_unique_id}}
* {{params_without}}
* {{nested_field_id}}
* {{nested_field_name}}

There’s also one function that is used, but is an exact copy of [a pre-existing Rails method|https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/3872bc0e54d32e8bf3a6299b0bfe173d94b072fc/actionview/lib/action_view/helpers/cache_helper.rb#L215-L223], so it doesn’t need to be redefined:

* {{cache_if}}

All of these methods can be deleted without affecting functionality.

The {{Pseud}} class has a number of unused scopes:

* {{on_works}}
* {{with_works}}
* {{with_posted_works}}
* {{with_public_works}}
* {{with_bookmarks}}
* {{with_public_bookmarks}}
* {{with_public_recs}}
* {{alphabetical}}
* {{starting_with}}

It also has some unused methods:

* {{visible_works_count}}
* {{visible_recs_count}}

They can all be safely deleted.

column149:
27/Sep/23 3:42 PM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;It looks like one thing we need to do is specify the emails for the creators in {{creatorship_notification_data}} to ensure they’re not being localized – I haven’t been able to produce the error since doing that, but it sounds like that’s just luck and there might be other things that need help as well.

ETA: Just to clarify the problem a bit more, here’s our [user factory|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/master/factories/users.rb]:

{noformat}  sequence :email do |n|
    Faker::Internet.email(name: "#{Faker::Name.first_name}_#{n}")
  end

  factory :user do
    login { generate(:login) }
    password { "password" }
    age_over_13 { "1" }
    terms_of_service { "1" }
    password_confirmation(&:password)
    email { generate(:email) }

    # By default, create activated users who can log in, since we use
    # devise :confirmable.
    confirmed_at { Faker::Time.backward }

    trait :unconfirmed do
      confirmed_at { nil }
    end{noformat}

The email is made up of a first name ({{Faker::Name.first_name}}) with a number appended ({{#{n}}}). The name will be in whatever locale you select when previewing the email. So, e.g., if you select zh-CN, it will be one of the [first names for zh-CN|https://github.com/faker-ruby/faker/blob/2f06350da484de1fbc3f2e8ef3f1c755502a7a45/lib/locales/zh-CN.yml#L21], and thus it will contain characters that [our email format validator|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/317e3e840ce28f1d66e2ede43fdbd3e6bf0218bd/app/validators/email_format_validator.rb#L10] doesn’t allow.

The easiest way around that is to specify account emails for any users we create for the mailer previews, but that’s probably going to get old. We might want to change the factory itself, maybe to something like a nice alphanumeric ({{Faker::Alphanumeric.alphanumeric(number: 10, min_alpha: 3, min_numeric: 3)}})?

ETA 2: Oh! I know why we see the error in different places. In locales like {{pt-BR}}, only some first names have characters that are invalid in AO3 email addresses. So it’s possible for the first creator to be valid, but the second creator to be invalid:

{noformat}  def creatorship_notification_data
    first_creator = create(:user, login: "JayceHexmaster")
    second_creator = create(:user, login: "viktor_the_machine")
    work = create(:work, authors: [first_creator.default_pseud, second_creator.default_pseud])
    second_creatorship = Creatorship.find_by(creation: work, pseud: second_creator.default_pseud)
    [second_creatorship, first_creator]
  end{noformat}

Whichever user has the invalid-to-us email is determines how the page errors. So if you set the email in both places (or fix the factory), then yeah, both types of 422 errors will clear up.

