column162:
20/Sep/23 4:26 AM;63039b5aed0c9b03372bf3c4;{quote}❓ Deleted Account 1: The 'added as FNOK' line disappeared from the account history of Account 3 (the then-current FNOK), so no trace remains that Account 3 was ever an FNOK. Nothing was added. I don't think that should happen?{quote}

Indeed, a side effect of only logging changes in the database on the side of the user with a fnok, thus losing the data if they are deleted.

[https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/pull/4633|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/pull/4633|smart-link] should fix that.

column150:
10/Feb/23 5:57 PM;557058:fa089759-8cd1-4db3-b506-5731a37fd5a6;{quote}But the message included “Email is already part of our queue” twice. Not a dealbreaker, imo.{quote}

This is because that error is generated once by the [normal uniqueness check|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/850b858b8034bd44d5fb851f2cfbfec3ae3766b0/app/models/invite_request.rb#L5], and once by the [check for simplified uniqueness|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/850b858b8034bd44d5fb851f2cfbfec3ae3766b0/app/models/invite_request.rb#L17-L22]. (So actually, I guess the QA step for this should specify that you _need_ to see two copies of the same message — only one error message would indicate that the normal uniqueness check wasn’t working properly.)

{quote}Although the error did stick around for a page or two after I navigated away.{quote}

That’s the classic [flash error + render issue|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/850b858b8034bd44d5fb851f2cfbfec3ae3766b0/app/controllers/pseuds_controller.rb#L97-L99]. But actually, looking at that code, the QA step that I wrote isn’t testing the right thing, because it’s getting caught by the controller check instead of the model check. So I think I need to edit that step to say “Create two pseuds for the same account, then edit the second pseud so that it has the same name as the first but with different capitalization. Make sure you get a ‘Name has already been taken’ error.”

{quote}But I also get a third not mentioned here: Email is invalid.{quote}

That’s from [Devise|https://github.com/heartcombo/devise/blob/8e2e3f6fda1ceabe2eff08cf34a6e663527c438b/lib/devise/models/validatable.rb#L32-L38] — the code producing that error didn’t change, so it doesn’t need to be checked, but it’s normal.

{quote}I got the message from the above, too. Sort of makes sense, though: if it’s not valid it won’t be reachable.{quote}

Yeah, that’s normal. Maybe not a good thing, since it seems pointless to try to connect to a URL that you know is invalid, but I think that’s the way the code works in production as well.

EDIT: I edited the steps to try to make it easier for the next person tasked with testing this.

