09/May/23 12:50 AM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;{quote}question: should “Please use this form for …" be <h3 class="heading"> to match Support’s form? {quote}

No, the {{h3}} on Support's form actually needs to be removed the next time that page is changed -- the text in question doesn't function as a heading, so it shouldn't be marked up at such.

{quote}(I also notice that in features/other_a/abuse_report.feature something got changed to match the email description on the form, but I think that bit is referring to the error message?) {quote}

Yeah, good catch -- that should probably be changed back.

{quote}I’m not really sure why it broke in the middle like that on desktop (it’s one line on mobile, and even on desktop there was room for it to wrap later than it did) but it still looks … “off”, I guess. I do think bold and/or no paragraph break would be more cohesive.{quote}

So it’s actually coded to be on the same line, but the pesky {{p}} tags you see in [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-6519|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-6519|smart-link] are causing weirdness here (probably also relates to the odd breaking--paragraph tags aren’t meant to be nested, but here they are, so there’s no telling how a given mail client or browser will handle it):

{noformat}<p>
     <%= style_work_metadata_label(t(".copy.summary")) %>
     <%= raw @summary %>
</p>{noformat}

Which leads to this HTML in the email:

{noformat}  <p>
    <b style="color:#990000">Terms of Service violation: </b>
    <p>The summary</p>
  </p>{noformat}

We should probably reformat it as plain text for now, like we do in the text email instead: {{<%= to_plain_text(raw @summary) %>}}

