column30:
We’d like to [disallow OpenAI’s GPTBot|https://platform.openai.com/docs/gptbot/disallowing-gptbot] in our robots.txt.

We can also get rid of these lines in the Googlebot section:

{{Disallow: /*?*selected_pseuds}}

{{Disallow: /*?*use_openid}}

{{Disallow: /people?*show=}}

*Testing*

Once it’s in place on production, check robots.txt and confirm the new rule is there and the old ones are gone. (Staging uses a different robots.txt file, plus Systems has to update this, iirc; it’s not automatically done on deploy.)

We have a very slow exists query when creating or updating challenge sign-ups:

{{SELECT ? AS one FROM `tags` INNER JOIN set_taggings ON set_taggings.tag_id = tags.id}}
{{ INNER JOIN tag_sets ON tag_sets.id = set_taggings.tag_set_id}}
{{ INNER JOIN owned_tag_sets ON owned_tag_sets.tag_set_id = tag_sets.id}}
{{ INNER JOIN owned_set_taggings ON owned_set_taggings.owned_tag_set_id = owned_tag_sets.id}}
{{ INNER JOIN prompt_restrictions ON (prompt_restrictions.id = owned_set_taggings.set_taggable_id AND owned_set_taggings.set_taggable_type = ?) WHERE `tags`.`type` = ? AND (prompt_restrictions.id = ?) LIMIT ?}}

This is most likely from [this line in the prompt model|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/801ce0931c0dbb144f9631eed5e9cf743a6c8d00/app/models/prompt.rb#L125], which checks whether the challenge has any tag sets containing each type of tag, regardless of whether the challenge actually allows that type of tag (e.g., it checks for tag sets containing relationships even if your challenge only allows fandoms) or the prompt contains that type of tag (e.g., if the challenge allows additional tags and you leave the additional tags field blank, it still checks for tag sets containing additional tags).

We should:

* stop performing unnecessary checks
* improve the indices, if possible

*Testing*

Create a challenge that requires you to use tags from its tag set and make sure it errors if you try to use a tag not in the tag set.

*Steps to Reproduce*

# Submit a PR that only updates either test files or files within .github/workflows/
## Alternatively, you can submit a PR changing anything, and just add a label like {{Scope: Tests Only}}
# Ask someone to change the label from {{Awaiting Review}} to {{Reviewed: Action Needed}}
# Push another change to the file(s) you updated in step 1

*What Happens*

The labeler action removes the additional label, {{Scope: Tests Only}} and replaces {{Reviewed: Action Needed}} with {{Reviewed: Coder Has Actioned Review}}

*What Ought to Happen*

The labeler action should still change the {{Reviewed: ...}} labels, but it should retain any additional ones like {{Scope: Tests Only}}

The FAQs use the same [styling for definition lists|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/9705a64461a313afc0f0616d4ab0362c7d868309/public/stylesheets/site/2.0/21-userstuff.css#L15C1-L36C2] as works and other things written by users (e.g., bookmark notes):

{noformat}.userstuff dl {
  margin: 1.5em 0;
  border: 0;
}

[snip]

.userstuff dd {
  display: block;
  line-height: 1.5;
  margin-block-start: 1.25em;
  margin-block-end: 1.25em;
  margin-inline-start: 3em;
}{noformat}

But those margins are honestly kind of big! Compare to [that of ordered lists|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/9705a64461a313afc0f0616d4ab0362c7d868309/public/stylesheets/site/2.0/21-userstuff.css#L58-L69], for example:

{noformat}.userstuff ol {
  margin: 0.75em auto;
  padding: 0 1.5em;
}

.userstuff ol li {
  list-style-type: decimal;
  margin-block-start: 0.75em;
  margin-inline-end: 0;
  margin-block-end: 0.75em;
  margin-inline-start: 1.75em;
}{noformat}

So let’s shrink the margins for {{dl}} and {{dd}} when we’re in {{.faq .userstuff}}:

{noformat}.faq .userstuff dl {
  margin: 0.75em 0;
}

.faq .userstuff dd {
  margin-block-start: 0.75em;
  margin-block-end: 0.75em;
  margin-inline-start: 1.75em;
}{noformat}

*Testing*

Docs admins should make an FAQ answer with a {{dl}}, if there isn't one already, and confirm the new margins are better.

