column150:
30/Dec/23 7:07 PM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;[~accountid:629ad80c9bc7150068d02078] Oh, that’s tricky! And I suspect the answer is going to depend on the PDF reader you’re using. 😕 However, [Adobe has this to say about PDF fonts|https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/pdf-fonts.html]:

{quote}When a font cannot be embedded because of the font vendor’s settings, and someone who opens or prints a PDF does not have access to the original font, a *Multiple Master* typeface is temporarily substituted: AdobeSerifMM for a missing serif font, and AdobeSansMM for a missing sans serif font.{quote}

So that makes me think that, as long as we’re embedding the font, it shouldn’t matter for our purposes what is or isn’t on your system (at least, if you’re using Acrobat).

column155:
18/Feb/24 6:21 PM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;It looks like subsetting for CID fonts wasn’t added until version 6.17 of Calibre, so that might be the problem.🤞 You can Ctrl+F the [Calibre release notes|https://calibre-ebook.com/whats-new] for this bit:

{quote}Font subsetting: Add support for WOFF format fonts and CID keyed fonts. Also further reduce file sizes when subsetting{quote}

ETA: Brian was using Docker, which had Calibre 5.x like staging/production and was also failing to produce PDFs with font subsets. When they forced it to update to 7.x,  they confirmed subsetting worked, so it is indeed an issue with our version of Calibre. 🎉  They’re now wearing their Systems hat and looking into our options with regard to updating, since the reason we’re so far behind is the default Debian repo only goes up to 5.x.

